AG CuteMusic
============

Copyright (C) 2026 AG Projects. License: GPL-3.0-or-later.

Music player for a local music library, with:

- Podcast mixer for Blink calls: mic, music and the call mixed
- Streaming of libraries on the local network, or from a headless server
- Library consolidation: compilations, years, artist names, missing albums

PyQt6, GStreamer (python3-gi) and mutagen; Linux and macOS.

  bin/ag-cute-music [<files or folders>]
  bin/ag-cute-music --headless [--music <folder>] [--name <name>] [--port 47100] [--rescan <minutes>]


Libraries on the LAN
--------------------

File -> Share my library on the LAN offers the music folder, read only, to the other AG CuteMusic on the LAN
(HTTP on port 47100, announced by Bonjour with python3-zeroconf; else File -> Connect to a library... by
address). The button left of the views lists the libraries found: one picked fills the tree and is browsed and
played as the local one, each song fetched first (with its lyrics and images) into ~/.ag-cute-music/remote/.
Right-click -> Copy to this computer brings songs, albums or artists into the music folder as
<artist>/<album>/<file>; those already there are left. No password: for a trusted LAN.

Headless (a NAS, a server): --headless shares the folder with no windows, read again every 60 minutes (only
what changed). As a systemd user service: systemd/ag-cute-music-share.service. Needs python3-pyqt6 (QtCore),
python3-mutagen and python3-zeroconf only.


Organizer
---------

File -> Organize library... previews what it would change; only what is ticked is done, then the library is
read again.

- Compilations: an album spread over a folder per artist (as iTunes filed one without its compilation flag)
  gathered in Compilations/<album>, optionally tagged as one (album artist "Various Artists").
- Years: album folders renamed "YYYY - Album" from their tracks' year.
- Unknown albums: songs without one looked up online (MusicBrainz, else the iTunes Store) by artist and title;
  the album and year written in their tags, optionally moved into <artist>/<album>.
- Artists: names that look like one artist written differently ("De Phazz", "de-phazz", "Krall Diana", all
  lowercase) made one, in the tags, optionally their folders merged.
- Duplicates: the same song in several files, shown only.

Files are moved, never overwritten; .lrc/.txt and folder images go along, empty folders are removed. Every
change is in ~/.ag-cute-music/organizer.log, each run's moves in organized-<time>.json (to undo by hand).


Mixer and Blink
---------------

View -> Mixer (Ctrl+5): a small console. MIC: the input, echo, fader and meter; TO MONITOR hears it in the
headset (off during a call, not to hear oneself). MUSIC: VOCAL CUT (the singer taken out, karaoke), TO CALLS (the
music sent into Blink's call too, with the mic), its fader and meter. BLINK, only while Blink runs: who is in its
call and what it plays, on our output with its own fader, never sent back into the call. POWER off: no device for
Blink, no mic.

In Blink, Devices -> AG Podcast Mixer, one device for input and output (Blink's echo cancelling off on it), always
there: silent while the mixer is off (python3-sipsimple docs/PodcastMixerLink.md):

- macOS: the AG Podcast Mixer driver, installed from AG CuteMusic's Play menu.
- Linux: PipeWire nodes installed by the package (/usr/share/pipewire/pipewire.conf.d/ag-podcast-mixer.conf, read at
  the next login): the mix played into ag_podcast_mixer_feed comes out of ag_podcast_mixer, Blink's microphone; Blink
  plays the call into ag_podcast_mixer_call, the BLINK strip. Blink's SIP audio reaches them through the ALSA PCM
  ag_cutemusic_mixer (alsa/60-ag-cute-music.conf, in /etc/alsa/conf.d), its WebRTC rooms by node name.

Blink's calls on the BLINK strip, and its HANG UP and REDIAL, go over a control channel: the driver's properties on
macOS, D-Bus on Linux (org.agprojects.PodcastMixer).

Negatives (karaoke)
-------------------

A negative is a song without its singer, made offline by a neural separation model (BS-RoFormer, through
audio-separator): far cleaner than the mixer's VOCAL CUT. Right-click songs, an album or an artist in the Library,
or a playlist in Playlists -> Make negative: they are queued and made one at a time in the background (minutes a
song on a CPU, much less on a GPU: NVIDIA's, or Apple Silicon's), the queue kept across restarts (the song being
made when quitting starts over).

The Negatives tab, after the Library and as it is, shows the negatives made, and the ones being made, queued or
failed dimmed with their state (right-click: Remove from the queue, Try again, Stop, Start). Playing a negative
shows the Lyrics view; playing a song that has a negative (or a negative), a button at the bottom left of the view
(not on the Mixer) goes on with the other one from the same point. View -> Negatives (Ctrl+K) shows the tab.

They are FLACs next to the music folder, in a folder of the same name and "-Negatives" (~/Music/Player:
~/Music/Player-Negatives), at the songs' own paths, with their tags, cover and lyrics (the lyrics found online are
the songs').

The separator is installed on first use into ~/.ag-cute-music/separator (a venv of the system's python3:
python3-venv; PyTorch and audio-separator, about 3 GB, the model 640 MB in ~/.ag-cute-music/separator-models).
It reads and writes the songs with ffmpeg (apt install ffmpeg; on macOS brew install ffmpeg).

File -> Web libraries... -> Enable serves a web page (port 47101) where others on the LAN send songs under a token
of 5 digits, play them, and get their negatives once started here (the Web tab). All of it: doc/negatives.md.


Docked
------

View -> Dock at the right (Ctrl+D): a frameless column on top, a fifth of the screen's width and its full
height, with the cover, the VU meter, the lyrics and the transport; the main window hidden meanwhile. Esc, a
double click or right-click -> Undock bring it back. Keys, media keys and the wheel (volume) work there too.
Quit docked, it starts docked (on the screen under the mouse).

View -> Full screen (Ctrl+F; Cmd+F on macOS): a kiosk, the visualizer and the play bar on the whole screen, no title bar, menu bar
or library; the keys work as ever. Esc or Ctrl+F brings the window back as it was.


Also
----

- Edit metadata (right-click a song, album or selection): year, artist, album, genre; "Multiple values" left
  alone unless typed in.
- Move to Trash (Delete): into <music folder>/.ag-cute-music-trash, in the same layout; File -> Empty Trash
  deletes for good.
- Amplifier view: meters (a lever: analog needles, circle or spectrum), volume (to 11: up to +6 dB past the top,
  clipping included), bass, treble and balance (equalizer-3bands, audiopanorama), kept in the settings.
- Lava lamp (a VU meter style): the wax heated, rising and sinking, faster with the music's loudness, swelling with its bass;
  a click changes its colours.
- Playlists: .m3u8 in ~/.ag-cute-music/playlists, plus those in the music folder; File -> Import playlists...
  takes .m3u/.pls files or the Library.xml exported by iTunes / Music.
- Resumes where it was left (queue, track, position), paused. Gapless.
- Cover: the file's picture, else cover/folder/front.jpg next to it, else online. Lyrics: <file>.lrc/.txt,
  else the tags, else online; synced ones follow the song.
- Media keys without the focus: MPRIS on Linux (org.mpris.MediaPlayer2.agcutemusic, also playerctl).

Not obvious keys: Ctrl+1..5 the views (cover, lyrics, VU meter, amplifier, mixer; the lava lamp is a VU meter style), Ctrl+Shift+1..4 by year, artist,
album, folder, Ctrl+L the playing track in the tree, Ctrl+S the search field (Space plays and pauses while it is empty),
Ctrl+Shift+S shuffle, Shift+Left/Right 10 s back/forward, F5 rescan. On macOS Ctrl is
Cmd, except Control+Tab the next view and Cmd+Option+1..4 by year, artist, album, folder (Cmd+Shift+3..5 are the
system's screenshots).


Files
-----

  ~/.ag-cute-music/libraries/   the tags cache, one file per music folder (a rescan reads only what changed)
  ~/.ag-cute-music/queue.json   the queue and the point in it;  history.json  the last 1000 plays
  ~/.ag-cute-music/ui.conf      settings;  cutemusic.log, console.log  the logs
  kill -USR2 <pid>              where every thread is, into console.log (a hang)

Environment: CUTEMUSIC_AUDIO_SINK=<element [properties]> forces the output ("alsasink device=hw:1"); else the
first that opens of osxaudiosink, pulsesink, pipewiresink, alsasink, autoaudiosink. A named output listed by
both PipeWire and PulseAudio plays through pulsesink (pipewiresink deadlocks seeking while paused).
On Wayland it runs through XWayland; CUTEMUSIC_WAYLAND=1 runs it natively.


Dependencies
------------

Debian and Ubuntu packages: http://packages.ag-projects.com (apt install ag-cute-music).

Debian/Ubuntu: python3-pyqt6 python3-gi python3-mutagen gir1.2-gstreamer-1.0 gir1.2-gst-plugins-base-1.0
gstreamer1.0-plugins-base gstreamer1.0-plugins-good, gstreamer1.0-pulseaudio or gstreamer1.0-pipewire;
gstreamer1.0-libav and gstreamer1.0-plugins-ugly for AAC/ALAC and the rest; python3-zeroconf for sharing.

  ./makedeb.sh [distro] [rebuild|install]     dist/ag-cute-music_<version>_all.deb

macOS: see macos/README (bash macos/install.sh makes the app).


